{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>For the cloud of YHWH was upon the tabernacle by day, and fire was in it by night.</b> It would have been fitting for Scripture to say 'a cloud by day (ביום) and fire by night (בלילה)'; rather, it comes to allude to the attribute of day and the attribute of night. Know that the essential word is יום, but the mem was doubled, for thus the attribute of mercy is a doubled word — מום (מלה כפולה); and the word לילה — the essential word is ליל, and the final he alludes to the attribute of judgment, whose dominion prevails at night." "<b>And already</b> you know that it was drawn out against the firstborn of Egypt at midnight, and this is the secret of the language in the words of Scripture; and so too what is written (Joshua 1): ״והגית בו יומם ולילה״ — it would have been fitting to say והגית בו ביום ובלילה." "<b>But rather</b> it comes to allude that the scroll of the Torah is comprised of mercy and judgment — the Written Torah and the Oral Torah." "<b>From here</b> you will understand the clinging of the Shekhinah, for there they were as one in the wilderness, dwelling in tents, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 33): ״ונצבו איש פתח אהלו״, and it is written (Numbers 24): ״מה טובו אהליך״. Now, above he mentioned ״בני ישראל״ because he mentioned there ״ענן״, and it is the cloud of YHWH; but here he mentioned ״כל בית ישראל בכל מסעיהם״ because he mentioned the cloud and the fire. And how fair were the banners of Israel in the wilderness — holy camps — equivalent to the camp of angels and tarshishim, with the Shekhinah visible in the cloud to all of them, men and women. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Psalms 85:10): ״אך קרוב ליראיו ישעו לשכון כבוד בארצנו״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: In this world I caused My Shekhinah to dwell among you in the Temple, and because of your sins it was withdrawn from you; but in the time to come it shall not budge from you, as it is said (Leviticus 26:11–12): ״ונתתי משכני בתוככם ולא תגעל נפשי אתכם והייתי לכם לאלהים״ — end of quotation. And the explanation of the matter is that in the future prophecy shall return to Israel as of old, and the Shekhinah to its station and its foundation; all shall learn the knowledge of HaShem with an abundance of wisdom, and they shall no longer learn war; a new heart shall be created and a heart of stone shall be removed and torn away; there shall be no adversary and no evil inclination. And this is our consolation in this final redemption — that the splendor and the glory shall return to the midst of Israel as at first, for thus Isaiah of blessed memory promised at the close of his book in the choicest of his vision, in the words (Isaiah 60): ״כי בא אורך וכבוד ה'״. And if in the first redemption our King loved us — whose love for His beloved is better than wine — and He appeared to us in His glory through the sense of sight, through the partition of cloud in a land of desert and waste, and HaShem was there, in the manner of what is written (Numbers 14): ״אשר עין בעין נראה אתה ה'״ — surely in the time to come our apprehension shall be yet greater, and the glory shall be seen eye to eye, without a separating partition and without a cloud that is seen, as the prophet of blessed memory promised (Isaiah 52): ״קול צופיך נשאו קול יחדו ירננו כי עין בעין יראו בשוב ה' ציון״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {3 "The phrase 'מלה כפולה' (a doubled word) appears after 'מום'; the exact reference is uncertain and may be textually difficult. Rendered hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:08:17.318698873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}